WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.659
I get a lot of questions about
programming but I don't think they're

00:00:03.659 --> 00:00:07.799
the right questions to ask questions
like how many languages do I need to

00:00:07.799 --> 00:00:11.160
learn what language do I need to learn
to get to company X what's the

00:00:11.160 --> 00:00:15.389
difference between a and B and which
makes more money what are the exact

00:00:15.389 --> 00:00:20.430
steps I need to take to become Z what
kind of program should I write to build

00:00:20.430 --> 00:00:24.150
up my resume so why don't I like these
questions because it feels like the

00:00:24.150 --> 00:00:28.080
people who are asking these questions
they are learning how to program just

00:00:28.080 --> 00:00:32.910
for the sake of learning how to program
but programming is just a tool being a

00:00:32.910 --> 00:00:37.920
programmer for me means you write code
to automate things or to make things

00:00:37.920 --> 00:00:41.730
perform certain actions for example
programming a refrigerator to turn on

00:00:41.730 --> 00:00:45.420
the light when you open the door when
you think about it programming is

00:00:45.420 --> 00:00:49.680
actually kind of boring you're just
giving a series of instructions to a

00:00:49.680 --> 00:00:53.309
machine over and over again until you
get it right I don't want to speak for

00:00:53.309 --> 00:00:58.500
all of you but I think if the pay wasn't
great I don't think a lot of you would

00:00:58.500 --> 00:01:03.059
spend hours and hours every night in
front of your computer trying to learn

00:01:03.059 --> 00:01:06.780
how to code or to just debug your
program sometimes we get caught up

00:01:06.780 --> 00:01:11.189
learning languages or doing coding
challenges or fantasizing about you know

00:01:11.189 --> 00:01:15.689
working for these big tech companies
that we forget why we wanted to learn

00:01:15.689 --> 00:01:20.909
programming in the first place you
wanted to program because you saw the

00:01:20.909 --> 00:01:25.259
amazing things that can be built using
programming so don't just learn how to

00:01:25.259 --> 00:01:28.840
program build something that matters
build something that means

00:01:28.840 --> 00:01:33.219
build something that solves a problem
you're a problem-solver a creator an

00:01:33.219 --> 00:01:37.060
innovator you're not a programmer
programming is just a tool in your

00:01:37.060 --> 00:01:41.859
arsenal to build something amazing you
can be analytical creative empathetic

00:01:41.859 --> 00:01:45.939
and programming allows you to express
those qualities of yourself but

00:01:45.939 --> 00:01:50.860
programming in of itself is nothing
special it's like a pencil it can mark

00:01:50.860 --> 00:01:55.179
stuff down if you press on the pen so
hard enough that's it but with a pencil

00:01:55.179 --> 00:01:59.079
you can write novels draw beautiful
portraits build plans for a skyscraper

00:01:59.079 --> 00:02:02.110
or anything eliminated only by your
imagination

00:02:02.110 --> 00:02:07.450
you don't learn how to program to get
into Google you learn how to program to

00:02:07.450 --> 00:02:10.660
build something meaningful something
that helps real people with real

00:02:10.660 --> 00:02:15.290
problems
I'm partnered with IBM today to talk

00:02:15.290 --> 00:02:19.730
about a global initiative called call
for code which calls for developers to

00:02:19.730 --> 00:02:23.930
build something impactful and have a
positive change across the world through

00:02:23.930 --> 00:02:27.560
their code as you know there are
hundreds and hundreds of natural

00:02:27.560 --> 00:02:32.390
disasters every year like hurricanes
earthquakes floods volcanoes and

00:02:32.390 --> 00:02:37.190
wildfires those affect numerous lives
and causes tremendous damage to many

00:02:37.190 --> 00:02:41.420
families around the world if you strive
for real impact we desperately need you

00:02:41.420 --> 00:02:46.459
this is why 2018 call for code global
challenge is a competition that asks

00:02:46.459 --> 00:02:50.269
people like you to build solutions to
improve what we can do to reduce

00:02:50.269 --> 00:02:54.140
disruptive impact of these natural
disasters we need all kinds of

00:02:54.140 --> 00:02:57.769
technologies you like working on
blockchains build secure supply networks

00:02:57.769 --> 00:03:02.870
to help distribute aid to the people in
need you're like AI are NLP make BOTS to

00:03:02.870 --> 00:03:07.220
help real-time communications to them
you're into data science analyze and

00:03:07.220 --> 00:03:11.870
predict what the affected population
needs before they even ask for it every

00:03:11.870 --> 00:03:16.190
one of your project will matter and it
will be for an extremely good cause now

00:03:16.190 --> 00:03:21.889
of course it is still a competition so
they have a grand prize of $200,000 not

00:03:21.889 --> 00:03:26.030
only that the project the greatest
potential impact will be implemented

00:03:26.030 --> 00:03:30.200
with the help of IBM the new Linux
Foundation the UN Human Rights and the

00:03:30.200 --> 00:03:34.459
American Red Cross
sorry offers challenge so don't forget

00:03:34.459 --> 00:03:38.350
don't be a programmer be a problem
solver

00:03:38.350 --> 00:03:41.670
going to call for code

